From: Ferestre contributors
Subject: Check what the download writes, and stop when it fails

A streaming install trusted the package completely and reported success
whatever happened.

Names: every file name in the package -- from its segment metadata or its
NTFS directory -- is rebuilt from plain components before it is joined to
the destination. A name that is empty, absolute, carries a drive letter,
stream, NUL or `.`/`..` component is refused, and the whole install with
it, before anything is fetched. `run` and `download` apply the same rule.
An NTFS name holding a separator is refused where it is read.

Integrity: the package is fetched over https; a plain http CDN root from the
package service is asked for over https instead (XODUS_ALLOW_HTTP_CDN=1
keeps it). `XvdFile::parse_verified` checks the hash tree against the
header's top hash level by level, then the metadata pages (file table,
segment metadata, region table) against the tree; the NTFS pages are
checked as they are read, and every page of every file before it is
decrypted or written. A damaged page is fetched again, three times at most,
then the file fails. The header signature is not checked, so the header is
as trustworthy as the TLS connection it came over. The installed container
and `run` keep the unchecked `XvdFile::parse`.

Retries: a dropped connection is retried with a doubling wait (1 s to 30 s)
and given up on after five minutes without a good page; 4xx answers other
than 408 and 429, and a 200 to a range request, fail at once. Write errors
are returned instead of retried every 30 s forever. A resumed header
connection that delivers nothing is retried eight times, then fails.

Result: `streaming` and `extract` exit 1 when the licence is refused, the
disk is full, or any file fails; the first failing file stops the rest and
its partial output is deleted, and the container is committed only when
every file arrived. The commit flushes the filesystem, renames over the old
record and syncs the directory. An unreadable old record means "nothing
installed" rather than a panic, and a file the record lists is fetched
again if it is missing or the wrong length on disk. Network and sign-in
failures in the package lookup are reported instead of panicking, the
progress label is cut on a character boundary, and the final JSON progress
line is waited for before exiting.

